  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 18.001pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(50.16pt, 99.36pt, "Innovation ARIMA models application to predict pressure ")
  #line-at(50.16pt, 123.36pt, "variations in water supply networks with open-loop control. ")
  #line-at(50.16pt, 147.24pt, "Case study in Noja (Cantabria, Spain) ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(36pt, 163.68pt, "David Muñoz-Rodríguez ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 6pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(151.8pt, 161.16pt, "a")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(154.8pt, 163.68pt, ", Manuel J. González-Ortega ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 6pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(286.2pt, 161.16pt, "b")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(289.56pt, 163.68pt, ", María-Jesús Aguilera-Ureña ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 6pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(424.08pt, 161.16pt, "a")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(427.08pt, 163.68pt, ", Andrés Ortega-Ballesteros ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 6pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(553.8pt, 161.16pt, "a")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(556.8pt, 163.68pt, ", ")
  #line-at(36pt, 180pt, "Alberto-Jesus Perea-Moreno a ")
  #line-at(36pt, 196.32pt, "a Departamento de Física Aplicada, Radiología y Medicina Física, Universidad de Córdoba, Campus de Rabanales, ")
  #line-at(36pt, 212.64pt, "14071 Córdoba, Spain ")
  #line-at(36pt, 228.96pt, "b Department of Aerospace Engineering and Fluid Mechanics, University of Seville, 41013 Seville, Spain ")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 11.04pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(57.24pt, 260.04pt, "Abstract:")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(113.64pt, 260.04pt, "Water utilities are increasingly concerned about losses, leaks, and illegal connections in their ")
  #line-at(36pt, 285.36pt, "distribution networks. Pressure control is typically managed through pressure reducing valves (PRVs) with ")
  #line-at(36pt, 310.68pt, "electrically controlled actuators based on predefined tables according to the pressure at the critical point ")
  #line-at(36pt, 336pt, "control (CPC). This open-loop control method lacks direct feedback between the PRV and CPC, making it ")
  #line-at(36pt, 361.2pt, "challenging to distinguish whether pressure variations originate from normal head losses or abnormal net-")
  #line-at(36pt, 386.52pt, "work conditions. ")
  #line-at(57.24pt, 411.84pt, "Unlike traditional applications of ARIMA focused on water demand forecasting, this study explores its ")
  #line-at(36pt, 437.16pt, "novel use in pressure management within distribution networks, aiming to predict P3 (CPC) pressure based ")
  #line-at(36pt, 462.48pt, "on head losses across a defined hydraulic sector. To achieve this objective, a predictive model based on the ")
  #line-at(36pt, 487.8pt, "Box-Jenkins methodology and its variations is implemented to analyse time series data. An action path is ")
  #line-at(36pt, 513pt, "established to determine the optimal model—ARIMA, ARMA, ARMAX, etc.—which is subsequently validated ")
  #line-at(36pt, 538.32pt, "using real operational data from Noja, a coastal town in northern Spain characterized by significant seasonal ")
  #line-at(36pt, 563.64pt, "population fluctuations. By accurately forecasting CPC pressure, this system enhances the detection of ")
  #line-at(36pt, 588.96pt, "anomalous patterns, enabling more efficient network pressure management. The study demonstrates the ")
  #line-at(36pt, 614.28pt, "potential of advanced modelling techniques in optimizing water distribution networks, providing valuable in-")
  #line-at(36pt, 639.48pt, "sights to improve system efficiency, reliability, and sustainability in urban environments. ")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 11.04pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(57.24pt, 664.8pt, "Keywords: ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(117pt, 664.8pt, "Potable water, leakage,")
  #line-at(235.44pt, 664.8pt, "pressure management; pressure reducing valve; critical point con-")
  #line-at(36pt, 690.12pt, "trol; ARMAX model, sustainable cities, water-energy nexus. ")
]

// arXiv source page 2
#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(295.08pt, 808.32pt, "2 ")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 11.04pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(36pt, 90.48pt, "1. Introduction ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(61.56pt, 125.64pt, "The proper and right management of water resources is gaining more and more importance over the ")
  #line-at(36pt, 158.28pt, "last years since it is required to secure drinking water supply to the growing world population, which, in many ")
  #line-at(36pt, 190.92pt, "cases, is concentrated in water scarce areas or places with less water available [1-4]. One of the main pillars ")
  #line-at(36pt, 223.44pt, "to secure water supply management is leakage control to avoid water losses [5-7].  ")
  #line-at(61.56pt, 256.08pt, "The International Water Association (IWA) differentiates four key points in a leakage policy management ")
  #line-at(36pt, 288.72pt, "strategy: active leakage control, asset management, speed and quality of repairs and pressure management ")
  #line-at(36pt, 321.24pt, "[8]. Pressure management has proven to be the most effective method for reducing and preventing the ap-")
  #line-at(36pt, 353.88pt, "pearance of new leaks in drinking water distribution piping systems due to the direct relationship between the ")
  #line-at(36pt, 386.52pt, "working pressure in the network and the water lost through leaks, as well as the appearance of new leaks ")
  #line-at(36pt, 419.04pt, "[9,10]. Thus, controlling the pressure in the networks allows to manage leakage level [11,12]. The relationship ")
  #line-at(36pt, 451.68pt, "between the ratio of pressure reduction in the network and leakage decrease depends on several factors, ")
  #line-at(36pt, 484.32pt, "such as pipe material and the pressure reduction level [13,14]. Moreover, pressure management is also ")
  #line-at(36pt, 516.84pt, "employed to manage customer demand an ensure adequate water supply throughout the day, though this ")
  #line-at(36pt, 549.48pt, "application is not so widely extended [15,16]. ")
  #line-at(61.56pt, 582.12pt, "The most common way to apply pressure management is a water distribution network is by the use of ")
  #line-at(36pt, 614.64pt, "pressure reducing valves (PRV) [17,18,4,19]. The function of a pressure reducing valve is to maintain a fixed ")
  #line-at(36pt, 647.28pt, "downstream pressure independent of flow variations or upstream pressure oscillations occurring in the net-")
  #line-at(36pt, 679.92pt, "work. Over the last years, electronic control systems and algorithms, with different levels of complexity, have ")
  #line-at(36pt, 712.44pt, "been introduced and developed to optimize the performance of these valves [20]. In this respect, it must be ")
  #line-at(36pt, 745.08pt, "added that recently pumps as turbines (PAT) have also become an optimal and sustainable alternative to ")
  #line-at(36pt, 777.72pt, "PRVs [21] since they also meet energy saving targets. And other authors such as Fontana and Marini (2021) ")
]

// arXiv source page 3
#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(295.08pt, 808.32pt, "3 ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(36pt, 100.44pt, "propose an approach to integrating hydropower generation with pressure-reducing valves in water distribu-")
  #line-at(36pt, 132.96pt, "tion networks. Instead of dissipating excess head through PRVs, this approach generates power. Utilising a ")
  #line-at(36pt, 165.6pt, "programmable logic controller (PLC), they address the optimisation problem directly, circumventing the ne-")
  #line-at(36pt, 198.24pt, "cessity for external modules [22].  ")
  #line-at(61.56pt, 230.76pt, "These pressure control valves have different strategies for pressure management: fixed outlet pressure, ")
  #line-at(36pt, 263.4pt, "time-based, flow-based, and critical point control (CPC). Of all of them, CPC is the most advanced, the one ")
  #line-at(36pt, 296.04pt, "that achieves a control more adjusted to the dynamics of the network, but it is also the most complex [23,24]. ")
  #line-at(36pt, 328.56pt, "The aim of CPC systems is keeping the pressure in the area as low as possible to reduce leakages, while ")
  #line-at(36pt, 361.2pt, "assuring the right level of supply to customers. Under CPC, the PRV is adjusted dynamically with different ")
  #line-at(36pt, 393.84pt, "values at the outlet to maintain a fixed pressure at the critical point of the area supplied [25]. CPC systems ")
  #line-at(36pt, 426.36pt, "can be classified into open or closed loop, depending on whether there is real time connection between the ")
  #line-at(36pt, 459pt, "critical point and the pressure reducing valve or not [26,27,4]. In critical point close loop, the critical point and ")
  #line-at(36pt, 491.64pt, "the PRV are communicating continuously to set the pressure value at the outlet of the valve according to the ")
  #line-at(36pt, 524.16pt, "pressure at the critical point. This type of systems requires infrastructure for real time communication between ")
  #line-at(36pt, 556.8pt, "the critical point and the PRV, which, in most of the cases, is expensive and difficult to commission. In open-")
  #line-at(36pt, 589.44pt, "loop systems, the pressure reducing valve and the critical point do not share real time signals and are based ")
  #line-at(36pt, 621.96pt, "on using statistical models and time series analysis, to forecast hydraulic performance of the network.  ")
  #line-at(61.56pt, 654.6pt, "In open-loop systems, the pressure reducing valve and the critical point do not share real-time signals ")
  #line-at(36pt, 687.24pt, "and are based on using statistical models and time series analysis to forecast the hydraulic performance of ")
  #line-at(36pt, 719.76pt, "the network. Several studies have explored the use of statistical techniques to detect and classify anomalies ")
  #line-at(36pt, 752.4pt, "in water distribution systems, distinguishing between events such as leaks, pipe bursts, and unauthorized ")
  #line-at(36pt, 785.04pt, "water usage. For instance, [55] proposed a method based on transform analysis in wireless sensor networks ")
]

