#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(292.68pt, 808.32pt, "25 ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(57.24pt, 90.48pt, "Also, the model would not apply in areas which are not well isolated or where the boundaries are ")
  #line-at(36pt, 115.68pt, "changing continuosly. This would involve major changes in the flow and thus the headloss between the outlet ")
  #line-at(36pt, 141pt, "of the pressure reducing valve and the critica point of the area.  ")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 11.04pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(36pt, 166.32pt, "4. Conclusions ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(57.24pt, 191.64pt, "One of the main issues in potable water distribution networks is leak management. A thorough ")
  #line-at(36pt, 216.96pt, "understanding of the network and effective sectorization are fundamental tools for companies in the sector ")
  #line-at(36pt, 242.28pt, "to manage their assets properly. In recent years, water management companies have been making significant ")
  #line-at(36pt, 267.48pt, "efforts to reduce leakage rates and ensure long-term supply to the population due to the challenges posed ")
  #line-at(36pt, 292.8pt, "by climate change, population growth, and water scarcity experienced in recent years. ")
  #line-at(57.24pt, 318.12pt, "Leak management has a dual perspective: on the one hand, the economic loss that these unbilled water ")
  #line-at(36pt, 343.44pt, "losses represent for public, private, or mixed companies. On the other hand, it represents a waste of a non-")
  #line-at(36pt, 368.76pt, "renewable resource, especially during drought periods as experienced in recent years in Spain due to climate ")
  #line-at(36pt, 393.96pt, "change. There are various tools to detect leaks in distribution networks, and as seen in this study, pressure ")
  #line-at(36pt, 419.28pt, "management is a very interesting parameter for predicting potential network leaks. Such tools are useful in ")
  #line-at(36pt, 444.6pt, "systems like the one analyzed in Noja, using open-loop control, where the use of predictive models generates ")
  #line-at(36pt, 469.92pt, "considerable interest among network management companies. ")
  #line-at(57.24pt, 495.24pt, "As analyzed in the study, pressure management, achieved through pressure reducing valves (PRVs), ")
  #line-at(36pt, 520.56pt, "plays a crucial role in reducing and preventing water loss in distribution piping systems. By controlling ")
  #line-at(36pt, 545.76pt, "pressure levels, leakage rates can be effectively managed, ensuring optimal water supply and distribution. ")
  #line-at(36pt, 571.08pt, "Critical point pressure control systems, particularly those employing open-loop approaches, offer ")
  #line-at(36pt, 596.4pt, "sophisticated solutions for maintaining optimal pressure levels at critical network points. ")
  #line-at(57.24pt, 621.72pt, "Different models based on the Box & Jenkins methodology and its variants have been analyzed. This ")
  #line-at(36pt, 647.04pt, "article proposes a new open-loop critical point system in which ARMA and ARMAX models are constructed ")
  #line-at(36pt, 672.24pt, "and validated to overcome the difficulties of having real-time pressure data at the system's critical point. The ")
  #line-at(36pt, 697.56pt, "best model is generated using the pressure at the PRV outlet and the flow signal as exogenous variables, ")
  #line-at(36pt, 722.88pt, "and the pressure level at the critical point as the dependent variable. The attempt to model the pressure data ")
  #line-at(36pt, 748.2pt, "series at the critical point without considering exogenous factors was rejected as it did not meet the Ljung-")
  #line-at(36pt, 773.52pt, "Box criterion. Among all the selected ARMAX models, those with the highest value for the AKAIKE criterion, ")
]

